  <section class="cw-story-hero page-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Radio / CW</p>
    <h1>The test I once failed became the mode I chose.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      I first met Morse code as a gate I had to clear. Nearly three decades
      later, I came back to it by choice—and found a mode that fits how I want
      to operate outdoors.
    </p>
    <div class="cw-story-summary" aria-label="CW journey summary">
      <div>
        <strong>April 12, 2026</strong>
        <span>First CW QSO</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>April 26, 2026</strong>
        <span>First all-CW activation</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <strong>1,621</strong>
        <span>CW QSOs through July 26</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="story-section cw-story-opening" aria-labelledby="cw-gate-title">
    <div class="story-marker" aria-hidden="true">
      <span>Then</span>
      <strong>1998</strong>
    </div>
    <div class="story-copy">
      <p class="section-label">The gate</p>
      <h2 id="cw-gate-title">I learned the tape instead of the code.</h2>
      <p>
        The first time I took the amateur radio exams, I aced the Technician
        written test and failed the Morse element. I had listened to the same
        Gordon West cassette so many times that I knew what came next, but I
        could not reliably recognize the characters outside that sequence.
      </p>
      <p>
        I went back, learned enough code to pass, and earned KB9SMK. Morse was
        compulsory, though, and that mattered. Once the test was behind me, I
        did not keep using it. By the time I returned to radio in 2025, I had
        forgotten essentially all of it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-block wide" aria-labelledby="cw-return-title">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="section-label">The return</p>
      <h2 id="cw-return-title">First, learn what the radio can hear.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>
        My wife gave me a Yaesu FTDX10 for Christmas 2025. It was a remarkable
        jump from carrying the old FT-100D between home and POTA activations,
        but the controls also made clear how much I did not know. Doug,
        N4HNH, had an extensive library of videos built around that radio. His
        practical sequence—work the signal from left to right, manage noise
        before adding gain, then narrow the filtering—gave me a mental model
        instead of a collection of buttons.
      </p>
      <p>
        Doug also chased SOTA stations on CW. Signals that seemed almost
        imaginary on voice could become workable when the receiver was narrowed
        around a Morse note. Watching him, Thomas K4SWL, Utah Forest KI7QCF,
        and Mike and Becky of Ham Radio Duo made CW look less like an exam and
        more like a portable operating skill.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-block wide" aria-labelledby="cw-system-title">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="section-label">The learning system</p>
      <h2 id="cw-system-title">Practice had to fit the life I actually had.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="split-list cw-learning-grid">
      <div>
        <h3>Protect one goal</h3>
        <p>
          I initially split my study time between CW and Amateur Extra and made
          little progress on either. Because Extra had a finish line, I focused
          on it first, set a deadline, and passed on March 5, 2026.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Use the drive</h3>
        <p>
          School runs and errands put me in the car for an hour or more most
          days. Morse Ninja's podcast lessons fit that time. I moved from 20 to
          30 WPM character speed so I would hear a sound rather than count dits.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Make repetition visible</h3>
        <p>
          Morse Mania helped with focused practice. The Vail tools added
          fifteen-minute daily sessions and a streak. By the end of that run I
          knew the letters and numbers well enough for CW Academy Fundamentals.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-block wide" aria-labelledby="cw-people-title">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="section-label">Elmers</p>
      <h2 id="cw-people-title">The most useful lesson was permission to be new.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>
        I joined the Long Island CW Club in August 2025, but did not immediately
        build much momentum. My brother Jon, KC1YED, learned the alphabet in
        Morse Mania and kept nudging me forward. The larger change came from
        operators who made the first live contact feel safe.
      </p>
      <p>
        On April 5, Brian K1NW heard one of my SSB POTA activations and realized
        he was nearby. He came to the park so we could work enough bands for his
        ninth N1CC award. When I mentioned that I was learning CW, he told me
        not to wait. Before leaving on a trip through the Southeast, he invited
        me to send my call whenever I heard him. He would slow down, repeat as
        much as needed, and make room for the contact.
      </p>
      <p>
        Thomas K4SWL later offered nearly the same advice: do not wait until it
        feels comfortable. Get on the air. Brian and Thomas were not dismissing
        the difficulty; they were separating difficulty from danger. A broken
        exchange would be recoverable.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cw-milestones" aria-labelledby="cw-milestones-title">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="section-label">The first six weeks</p>
      <h2 id="cw-milestones-title">One contact became a repeatable practice.</h2>
      <p>
        My log contains no CW before April 12. In May alone it contains 615 CW
        contacts.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <time datetime="2026-04-12">April 12</time>
        <div>
          <h3>The first QSO</h3>
          <p>
            At 23:47 UTC I heard K1NW activating Jones Lake State Park,
            US-2738, from grid FM04qq. I sent my call on 20 meters. Brian slowed
            down, and we completed the contact I had been postponing.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <time datetime="2026-04-14">April 14</time>
        <div>
          <h3>The other side of the exchange</h3>
          <p>
            I logged my first CW contact as the activator during a mixed-mode
            outing at Arcadia Management Area. Sending CQ was still a much
            larger step than answering it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <time datetime="2026-04-26">April 26</time>
        <div>
          <h3>First all-CW activation</h3>
          <p>
            At Scarborough North State Beach I made thirteen contacts—two by
            hunting and eleven from my own CQ—using five watts, a vertical on
            wet sand, and a speed I could control. This happened 54 days before
            the Florida-trip goal I had set for myself.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <time datetime="2026-04-27">April 27–30</time>
        <div>
          <h3>Repeat before the fear returns</h3>
          <p>
            I went back out three more times and logged 12, 11, and 25 CW
            contacts. Repetition turned a singular success into an operating
            mode.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <time datetime="2026-05-04">May 4–June 25</time>
        <div>
          <h3>CW Academy Fundamentals</h3>
          <p>
            Josep EA6BF led our twice-weekly group through the Fundamentals
            course. The class supplied structure, accountability, and other
            learners working through the same awkward moments.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <time datetime="2026-05-30">May 30–31</time>
        <div>
          <h3>Try the unreasonable thing</h3>
          <p>
            I spent roughly eight hours in CQ WPX CW and made 37 contacts,
            often with stations sending 35–40 WPM. Recording difficult
            exchanges and replaying them slowly was inelegant and effective.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <aside class="cw-story-quote" aria-label="What CW changed">
    <p>
      CW changed portable radio from talking at the landscape into sitting
      inside it. I can operate quietly enough for a family of deer to wander
      within thirty feet and never have to break the moment.
    </p>
  </aside>

  <section class="section-block wide" aria-labelledby="cw-current-title">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="section-label">Current edge</p>
      <h2 id="cw-current-title">A POTA exchange is not yet a conversation.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="story-prose">
      <p>
        POTA gave me exactly the right scaffold: recognize a call, exchange a
        report and location, log it, and do it again. I now enjoy CW activations
        far more than SSB, especially when I carry a small station down a trail.
        The compact exchange lets me recover from mistakes without also keeping
        track of a long social protocol.
      </p>
      <p>
        That scaffold is also the present limit. I have made only a couple of
        traditional contacts with names, locations, and ordinary conversation.
        I still lose track of what comes next. The goal is not a particular
        number on a speed test. It is being able to take a tiny rig in a side
        bag, throw a wire into a tree somewhere that is not a POTA park, call
        CQ, have a short human conversation, and pack up smiling.
      </p>
      <p>
        I am enrolled for the September–October CW Academy Intermediate term.
        I have also applied to CW Innovations and am waiting to hear back. The
        next phase is about conversational rhythm, head copy, and confidence
        when the exchange does not fit on a cheat sheet.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

